esults/' + $('#other_games_select').val();})"]] " | " [:a {:href "/event_names/42"} "Event type"]] [:h1 {:class "event_title"} "400 metres, Men" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "20 – 23 August 2004"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/779"} "Olympiako Stadio, Olympiako Athletiko Kentro Athinon Spiros Loues, Maroussi"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/66049"} "62 from 48 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "There were only three rounds, for the first time at the Olympics since 1912. The world leader coming into Athina was a relative newcomer, " [:a {:href "/athletes/105671"} "Jeremy Wariner"] ", who was trained by Clyde Hart, who had trained the redoubtable " [:a {:href "/athletes/78616"} "Michael Johnson"] ". Wariner also attended " [:a {:href "/affiliations/6372"} "Baylor University"] ", as had Johnson. The other Americans were " [:a {:href "/athletes/105653"} "Otis Harris"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/105663"} "Derrick Brew"] ", always to be considered challengers. But a top runner on the international circuit in 2004 was Grenada’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/70891"} "Alleyne Francique"] ". In the final, the Americans were in lanes 3-5, with Wariner in the middle. Harris led out for 200 metres, but the Americans were 1-2-3. At 300 metres Wariner and Harris were in a virtual dead-heat. But Wariner maintained more speed down the stretch and just over a metre, Harris in second, and Brew completing the American sweep. Françique placed fourth, never a factor for a medal after the first 200."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "SF"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "3359"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/10567